\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Our controlled ablation across two encoder architectures and four attack families identifies three findings about cross-attack generalization in multi-turn jailbreak detection. First, domain exposure via DAPT is the primary mechanism, bridging $\sim$60\,pp of the OOD gap on DeBERTa-v3 without any labels. Second, encoder pretraining method determines the difficulty threshold at which adaptation becomes critical: RoBERTa tolerates structurally simple attacks but fails on multi-persona ActorAttack (F1 0.981 $\to$ 0.466). Third, classification supervision functions as a representational regularizer, providing cross-seed stability (variance reduced 4--20$\times$ vs.\ MLM-only) and interpretable strategy characterization regardless of label correctness, though strategy predictions do not transfer to unseen attacks ($\kappa$=0.126). DAPT's detection advantage persists under adversarial paraphrasing (McNemar $p < 0.001$), though absolute perturbation robustness is attack-family-dependent. Validation on human-written ToxicChat jailbreaks confirms that the domain-adapted vs.\ vanilla gap transfers beyond synthetic data (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}); full multi-turn validation on human datasets is needed.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Dataset scale and diversity.}
The training set contains 500 synthetic conversations generated by a single model (Qwen3-8B), and OOD evaluation covers three attack families (FITD, Deceptive Delight, ActorAttack). This scale limits statistical power: the perturbation evaluation ($n$=76) means tier boundaries in Table~\ref{tab:perturbation} correspond to 1--3 misclassifications. Evaluation on ToxicChat (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}) confirms that the domain-adapted vs.\ vanilla gap transfers to human-written jailbreaks, partially addressing the single-LLM confound. However, ToxicChat contains only single-turn data; whether the full multi-turn evidence hierarchy (including classification supervision's stabilization benefit) generalizes to human-generated multi-turn attacks remains open.

